-- erwartet: N007
-- **The same overflow at the SISTER line, and it needed its own probe.**
--
-- `beispiele/gift/572` covers `umgebung.rs::typ_von_feld_mit`, which reads a `format` field.
-- A `device` register field takes a different route -- `typ_von_reg`, thirty lines down --
-- and it carried a byte-for-byte copy of the same arithmetic:
--
--     let w = (h.max(t) - h.min(t) + 1) as u32;
--
-- Measured 2026-09-01 against the unchanged checker, this file panicked at
-- `umgebung.rs:1364`, exit 101, while `586` panicked at `1334`. **Two lines, two probes** --
-- a single probe would have left whichever line it did not touch free to keep the defect,
-- and the two are far enough apart in the file that a repair to one does not draw the eye to
-- the other. *That is what happened when the line was written twice in the first place.*
--
-- WHY THE `reg` PATH LOOKED SAFE AND WAS NOT
-- ------------------------------------------
-- A `reg` carrier is always a literal integer type (`reg CTRL : u32`), never a path, so
-- `bitlage::wortbreite` cannot be walked past here the way it can at a `format` field --
-- `N007` covers every register field with no alias hole at all. That is exactly why the
-- crash was surprising: **the guard was complete and it still ran too late.** The
-- environment is built before the first pass, so a complete rule three passes downstream
-- protects nothing from arithmetic upstream of it.
--
-- The order is the whole finding. `N007` was never wrong; it was never asked.
module gift::registerbitbereich {

opaque type Pa = u64;

device Dev(basis : Pa) at mmio {
    reg CTRL : u32 @0x00 class rw fields { A @[340282366920938463463374607431768211455:0], }
}

}
